---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="A lecture theatre reduced to flat gold and black shapes, rows of seats sweeping past the frame, in a two-ink risograph print"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p class="central-question">
    Every week asks a version of one question: <strong>what makes a button
    feel worth pressing?</strong>
  </p>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    Take one real button apart before you're allowed to build one. Then build
    a button of your own, put it in front of someone else, and log exactly
    where it works and where it doesn't. The semester ends with one button
    you can defend, in full technical detail, against anyone in the room.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Third-year students who can already build a basic interactive web page,
    or something equivalent. No prior design background is assumed. What the
    course does assume is a willingness to take one small interaction apart,
    in detail, and justify every part of it.
  </p>

  <h2>How the semester moves</h2>
  <ol class="course-arc">
    <li><strong>Understand the button.</strong> What it is, and why a shape reads as pressable at all.</li>
    <li><strong>Study the press.</strong> Feedback, timing, and what happens on the other end of a push.</li>
    <li><strong>Test where it fails.</strong> Buttons that don't work, that go unseen, that stop working for a hand that isn't yours, that are built to be pressed too often.</li>
    <li><strong>Defend one perfect button.</strong> Build it, then argue for it.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>The weekly schedule and what happens in each teaching session.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>What is marked, what it is worth, and when it is due.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches the course and how to reach them.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .central-question {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
  }

  .course-arc {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
  }
</style>
